1 CONC ! Lester(Sylester) Brown Sthay was born in the small coal m
1 CONC ining and timber 
1 CONT town of Taylor, King County, Washington. The town was raze
1 CONC d when the Seattle
1 CONT Water Dept. acquired the land for their watershed on the Ce
1 CONC dar River.
1 CONT  The Sthay family moved to Seattle and lived in a house o
1 CONC n Denny Way, near the
1 CONT top of the hill. Upon completion of his home on 4th Ave an
1 CONC d NW 54th Sts.
1 CONT Grandfather moved the family in 1904. Lester served in th
1 CONC e U.S.Army Signal
1 CONT corp during WW I. He completed his training at Fort Monmout
1 CONC h,NJ. The war ended
1 CONT before he was scheduled to go overseas. He went to work fo
1 CONC r the Seattle
1 CONT Municipal Railway(Streetcar Co.) and continued on with th
1 CONC e Transit company
1 CONT until He retired in 1962, with 42 years of service. Being a
1 CONC s he was skilled as
1 CONT a carpenter and knew the entire City of Seattle, He entere
1 CONC d the Real Estate
1 CONT business and sold property until 1975. During the depressio
1 CONC n of the early 30's
1 CONT He augmented his very meager income of $14.00/week by doin
1 CONC g odd carprentry
1 CONT jobs for friends and neighbors.
1 CONT  He suffered an aneuorism of the Aorta in 1977 and was conf
1 CONC ined to a nursing
1 CONT home until his death in 1982.
